<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of the components of PPM.</span>
In Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ UGround: Towards Unified Visual Grounding with Unrolled Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, comparing Exp. 3, Exp. 6, we observe that dynamic layer selection yields a clear improvement (+2.07% gIoU and +5.02% cIoU
), highlighting the benefit of leveraging information across multiple layers. Moreover, the decomposition indicates that using the similarity map as a prompt (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math><sub id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sub">prompt</sub>) contributes the most (e.g., Exp. 1 vs. Exp. 2, and Exp. 4 vs. Exp. 5) within this paradigm.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T7" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:38%;">
